{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-81209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/state.json" "file_sha256" "17f114862f8bc295086bfd6ca75eb6d0dd2e240f3680f1e892861a2def005131"} {"question" "q-81209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-2)\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=-\\frac{4}{3},$ $c=\\frac{13}{3},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{(-\\frac{4}{3})}{(\\frac{13}{3})}=-\\frac{4}{13}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/state.json" "file_sha256" "17f114862f8bc295086bfd6ca75eb6d0dd2e240f3680f1e892861a2def005131"} {"question" "q-81209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9921c61e8a2def5f859b3c22e7e48c790ef5fc52270d26a70301c7efbd7d2a1d"} {"question" "q-81209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/state.json" "file_sha256" "17f114862f8bc295086bfd6ca75eb6d0dd2e240f3680f1e892861a2def005131"} {"question" "q-81209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{11}{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{8}"] ["math" "-\\frac{4}{13}"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/state.json" "file_sha256" "17f114862f8bc295086bfd6ca75eb6d0dd2e240f3680f1e892861a2def005131"} {"question" "q-81209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{4}{13}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/state.json" "file_sha256" "17f114862f8bc295086bfd6ca75eb6d0dd2e240f3680f1e892861a2def005131"} {"question" "q-86848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/state.json" "file_sha256" "17f114862f8bc295086bfd6ca75eb6d0dd2e240f3680f1e892861a2def005131"} {"question" "q-86848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 5+\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 0}{(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=\\frac{5}{2},$ $b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{5}{2})}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/state.json" "file_sha256" "17f114862f8bc295086bfd6ca75eb6d0dd2e240f3680f1e892861a2def005131"} {"question" "q-86848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9921c61e8a2def5f859b3c22e7e48c790ef5fc52270d26a70301c7efbd7d2a1d"} {"question" "q-86848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/state.json" "file_sha256" "17f114862f8bc295086bfd6ca75eb6d0dd2e240f3680f1e892861a2def005131"} {"question" "q-86848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\sqrt{7}}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/state.json" "file_sha256" "17f114862f8bc295086bfd6ca75eb6d0dd2e240f3680f1e892861a2def005131"} {"question" "q-86848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/state.json" "file_sha256" "17f114862f8bc295086bfd6ca75eb6d0dd2e240f3680f1e892861a2def005131"} {"question" "q-40738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/state.json" "file_sha256" "17f114862f8bc295086bfd6ca75eb6d0dd2e240f3680f1e892861a2def005131"} {"question" "q-40738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=2,$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/state.json" "file_sha256" "17f114862f8bc295086bfd6ca75eb6d0dd2e240f3680f1e892861a2def005131"} {"question" "q-40738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070290/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9921c61e8a2def5f859b3c22e7e48c790ef5fc52270d26a70